Day 20
-------

Collections
-----------
The Collection Interface is the root interface of the Java Collections Framework (except Map).
Collection -Used to represent group of elements/objects/data into single entity.
"collection" is an interface available in java.util

Java Collections Framework (Hierarchy)

                    Iterable
                        │
                        ▼
                  Collection (Interface)
          ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
          ▼             ▼             ▼
        List          Set          Queue
          │             │             │
   ┌──────┼──────┐   ┌──┼─────┐     ┌──┼─────┐
   ▼      ▼      ▼   ▼  ▼     ▼     ▼        ▼
ArrayList LinkedList Vector HashSet LinkedHashSet PriorityQueue ArrayDeque
                   │
                   ▼
                Stack

Map (Separate Interface)
      │
 ┌────┼───────────────┐
 ▼    ▼               ▼
HashMap LinkedHashMap TreeMap

LIST:
--------
It is used to store an ordered collection of elements, where:

Elements are stored in the order they are inserted.
Duplicate elements are allowed.
Elements can be accessed using their index.

1)ArrayList :
Uses a dynamic array internally.
Fast for searching and random access.
Slower for insertion/deletion in the middle.

1)Heterogenious data - allowed
2)Insertion order - preserved(Index)
3)Duplicate elements - allowed
4)multiple nulls - allowed


2)LinkedList
Uses a doubly linked list.
Faster insertion and deletion.
Slower random access.

syntax:List<String> list = new LinkedList<>();


3)Vector
Similar to ArrayList.
Synchronized (thread-safe).
Slower due to synchronization.

Syntax:List<String> list = new Vector<>();


SET:
------
The Set is a child interface of the Collection interface.

It is used to store unique elements, which means duplicate values are not allowed.

It belongs to the java.util package.

Features of Set:
 Does not allow duplicate elements.
 Stores unique elements only.
 Dynamic in size.
 Different implementations have different ordering behavior.
 No index-based access.

Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

Types of Set
1)HashSet

Features
Does not maintain insertion order.
Allows one null value.
Fastest among Set implementations.
Uses a Hash Table internally.

2)LinkedHashSet

Features
Maintains insertion order.
Allows one null.
Slightly slower than HashSet.

3)TreeSet

Features
Stores elements in sorted (ascending) order.
Does not allow null.
Slower than HashSet because it uses a Red-Black Tree.

MAP:
-----
A Map is an interface in the Java Collections Framework used to store data as key-value pairs.

Each key is unique, and it maps to a single value.

Note: Map is not a child of the Collection interface.

Features of Map
Stores data in key-value pairs.
Keys must be unique.
Values can be duplicated.
Each key maps to only one value.
Dynamic in size.


Types of Map
------------
1. HashMap

Features
Most commonly used.
Does not maintain insertion order.
One null key is allowed.
Multiple null values are allowed.
Fastest for searching, adding, and removing.

2. LinkedHashMap

Features
Maintains insertion order.
One null key allowed.
Multiple null values allowed.
Slightly slower than HashMap.

3. TreeMap
Features
Stores keys in ascending order.
Does not allow null keys.
Allows multiple null values.
Internally uses a Red-Black Tree.
